## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical firewall configuration skills on RHEL 9, requiring precise rule creation, persistence, and verification—essential for enterprise security administration.

## Key Concepts
- nftables ruleset syntax and packet flow
- Rule persistence via /etc/nftables.conf
- Service management with systemctl
- Connectivity testing from allowed vs. disallowed sources
- Verification using nft list ruleset

## Code Example
```bash
#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
flush ruleset
table inet filter {
  chain input {
    type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop;
    iifname "lo" accept
    ip saddr 192.168.1.0/24 tcp dport 22 accept
  }
  chain forward {
    type filter hook forward priority 0; policy drop;
  }
  chain output {
    type filter hook output priority 0; policy accept;
  }
}
```

## Implementation Steps
```bash
# Install nftables
dnf install -y nftables

# Create ruleset
cat > /etc/nftables.conf << 'EOF'
#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
flush ruleset
table inet filter {
  chain input {
    type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop;
    iifname "lo" accept
    ip saddr 192.168.1.0/24 tcp dport 22 accept
  }
  chain forward {
    type filter hook forward priority 0; policy drop;
  }
  chain output {
    type filter hook output priority 0; policy accept;
  }
}
EOF

# Enable and start services
systemctl enable nftables
systemctl start nftables
systemctl status nftables

# Verify rules
nft list ruleset

# Test connectivity (from allowed host)
ssh user@server-ip

# Test connectivity (from disallowed host)
ssh user@server-ip  # Should timeout/refuse
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical application of containerization, SELinux, and deployment orchestration on a single host with zero-downtime goals.

## Key Concepts
- Podman pod lifecycle and per-service user mapping
- SELinux policy customization for containerized apps
- Nginx TLS termination and blue/green traffic shifting
- Data migration, health checks, and rollback strategies

## Code Example
```bash
# Podman setup (illustrative)
sudo useradd -r apiuser
sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/api/data
podman pod create --name api-pod -p 8443:8443
podman run -d --name api --pod api-pod -v /var/lib/api/data:/data:Z --user apiuser:apiuser myrepo/api:latest
```

```selinux
# Minimal policy snippet outline (illustrative, not full policy)
module api_t 1.0;
require {
  type container_runtime_t;
  type api_data_t;
  class file { read write getattr };
  class dir { write read getattr };
  type self;
  class net_bind_service;
}
allow api_t api_data_t:dir { write read getattr };
allow api_t api_data_t:file { read write getattr };
# Allow binding to 8443 via runtime role
allow api_t self:net_bind_service;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to multiple hosts with a shared data backend and zero-downtime failover?
- What observability and metrics would you add to detect drift between blue/green environments?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical RHCSA skills applying to real-world multi-tenant TLS proxying, container runtimes, SNIs, firewall rules, SELinux, systemd upgrade churn, and test planning.

## Key Concepts
- Podman containers and nginx as TLS-terminating reverse proxy
- SNI-based routing, Per-tenant backends
- nftables rate limiting per tenant
- Vault integration and live cert reload
- Zero-downtime upgrades and SELinux policy

## Code Example
```
# example nginx snippet per tenant
server {
  listen 8443 ssl;
  server_name tenantA.example.com;
  ssl_certificate /vault/certs/tenantA.crt;
  ssl_certificate_key /vault/certs/tenantA.key;
  location / { proxy_pass https://10.0.3.21:8443; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test per-tenant rate limits under surge?
- How would you roll back a failed cert rotation?
